#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128838 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "07b63c0c-daaa-5f5b-acb3-58d6f36ee869" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Jump Discontinuity on a Graph"}] :question/id #uuid "ba2819ea-837b-5cfd-9475-8ed5a7bda000" :question/math-academy-id "e-3582" :question/problem "For which points does the function $y=f(x)$ plotted below have a jump discontinuity?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040492/assets/159566da49ef22ea1aae7800355949ae3cf02dac6a6a02e0a8bc344db176a75d.png)" :question/worked-solution "The function has a jump discontinuity at $x=0.$ Checking our conditions, we see that\n\n- $f(0) = -2$ exists $\\,\\,{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}$\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to 0}f(x)$ does not exist $\\,\\,{\\color{red}{\\times}}$\n\nSo the second condition failed. It failed because the left and right-sided limits are not equal:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 0^-}f(x) = -2, \\qquad \\qquad \\lim_{x\\to 0^+}f(x) = 2.\n$$\n\nThese limits both exist and are finite, yet they are not equal. Therefore, the discontinuity at $x=0$ is a jump discontinuity.\n\nNote that there are also discontinuities at $x=-3$ and $x=3,$ but they are removable discontinuities, *not* jump discontinuities."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128839 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d02297ca-0132-591b-9a69-bc2f93c95e2f" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Jump Discontinuities on a Graph when Multiple Types of Discontinuity Exist"}] :question/id #uuid "3607c9c1-25ca-5cfc-bd15-66e3b7e504c2" :question/math-academy-id "e-3583" :question/problem "For which points does the function $y=f(x)$ below have a jump discontinuity?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040492/assets/9e708e4fea2cd98e8d76978e58a4525d610d6f82521345b3aaa6232fd39b7539.png)" :question/worked-solution "The function has a jump discontinuity at $x=2.$ Checking our continuity conditions, we have\n\n- $f(2)= 1$ exists $\\,\\,{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}$\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to 2}f(x)$ does not exist $\\,\\,{\\color{red}{\\times}}$\n\nSo the second condition failed. It failed because the left and right-sided limits are not equal:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 2^-}f(x) = -1, \\qquad \\qquad \\lim_{x\\to 2^+}f(x) = 1.\n$$\n\nThese limits both exist and are finite, yet they are not equal. Therefore, the discontinuity at $x=2$ is a jump discontinuity.\n\nNote that there is also a discontinuity at $x=1,$ but this is not a jump discontinuity."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128848 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "485fd759-96b3-553f-9eaa-849878c30885" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Jump Discontinuity Given a Function Definition"}] :question/id #uuid "666caa80-54d7-5a3c-9ab9-1057e3fdeb90" :question/math-academy-id "e-3671" :question/problem "Does the function ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040492/assets/49e4cd87824a20b73175305aac7f3773f04a28e02cfa9c3c89cef9079a21ae8a.png) below have a jump discontinuity at ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040492/assets/22a0edba1f275de80719ea33fc4878640d2b2b93f8e5620c8999d6a449cdd35b.png)\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040492/assets/742066aa035c1b710ab30a74ff5320d3941aae17bbbb8a4c5edcf35ff67c5920.png)" :question/worked-solution "Let's go through our continuity checklist:\n\n- ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040492/assets/29d8d36954a25bff5808ce7c59dfc3ec0e373d22541cc2df85c93aa0a74c8bb4.png) exists ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040492/assets/a4785336954b0736113ea8ad968aa60db31e55720ff5bdeb128597a8c7c72b90.png)\n\n- Let's now check the left and right limits. We see that\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040492/assets/949d5bef0569fbd6f58382e07873412bb07cf3b735ae264c9cb78b366fa59bd2.png)\nwhile\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040492/assets/d6dfbdbb30c840068ad7a86370f6e444453f925ba8869140b7288b51d164f228.png)\nSo both the left and right limits exist and are finite, but they are not equal. ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040492/assets/81510388d37345ec3bfb525da6795c62d4ef190798e1574139469b5a90d17d69.png)\n\nTherefore, ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040492/assets/1d1ac18ca3b92f6df4647c49855e18d40ab7637269ff9b366edbfcaa96c4d59d.png) has a jump discontinuity at ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040492/assets/8f57be1d6e33e5ee1ad485bc58b29dbca4819ee229a505c0e7601150392d7615.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186307836 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "07b63c0c-daaa-5f5b-acb3-58d6f36ee869" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Jump Discontinuity on a Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dafacdb6-73cf-5171-8c8e-6efd7f497481" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040492/assets/131749caa4d5fd14d6cd58802b5e8bd6662c233f6a27f2317d491cbf3ab1f4d7.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307838} {:answer/id #uuid "0e7f87b8-8e7f-55e4-aff4-28d151448828" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040492/assets/6da963046fb53c6cdf84377657738f4c3c9d8c88c8df241b265fa2716ba6a03c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307839} {:answer/id #uuid "6552379e-23b8-5ee9-b1bd-73e67d66f733" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040492/assets/3811fcaaf0bbf2158a046dca5c8dc232fd268bce664c810df9fe114a404296cf.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307840} {:answer/id #uuid "b8ed649e-e1b8-5f71-b074-06ba434f2e6c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040492/assets/475ef6db32cd243d3f867f67c2a1feed2d64d087dff85d29cebac6cf10b2dcb0.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307841} {:answer/id #uuid "c7d3369b-924c-53df-aaeb-69db150e7229" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040492/assets/7f0230d9755ecc7db46f99f05d0c19bc280285cce0bbee2aab8dff540b207de4.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307842}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6552379e-23b8-5ee9-b1bd-73e67d66f733" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040492/assets/3811fcaaf0bbf2158a046dca5c8dc232fd268bce664c810df9fe114a404296cf.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307840} :answer-field/id #uuid "7b0b2ede-84bb-5de5-b1dd-f04ca5cbb5c4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307837}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d11a455c-5d50-5b38-8106-9406605cbcaa" :question/math-academy-id "q-104577" :question/problem "Which of the following functions has a jump discontinuity at $x=-3?$" :question/worked-solution "The function $y=g(x)$ has a jump discontinuity at $x=-3.$ Checking our conditions, we see that\n\n- $g(-3)=-2$ exists $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to (-3)}g(x)$ does not exist $\\,\\,\\times $\n\nSo the second condition failed. It failed because the left and right-sided limits are not equal:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (-3)^{-}}g(x)=-2,\\,\\,{lim}_{x\\to (-3)^{+}}g(x)=1.\n$$\n\nThese limits both exist and are finite, yet they are not equal. Therefore, the discontinuity at $x=-3$ is a jump discontinuity.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040492/assets/3811fcaaf0bbf2158a046dca5c8dc232fd268bce664c810df9fe114a404296cf.png)\n\nNotice that $h(x)$ and $p(x)$ are continuous at $x=-3,$ while $f(x)$ and $q(x)$ both have a discontinuity at $x=-3,$ but they are *not* jump discontinuities."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186307843 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d02297ca-0132-591b-9a69-bc2f93c95e2f" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Jump Discontinuities on a Graph when Multiple Types of Discontinuity Exist"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "22cc68e7-a8d6-5d4a-a344-563e3fab6fdc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-1$ and $x=2$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307845} {:answer/id #uuid "b3adf4dd-1f02-5245-9046-dc54088264f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=2$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307846} {:answer/id #uuid "392f495e-c19a-56f7-bbdf-0e99e267521d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=2$ and $x=4$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307847} {:answer/id #uuid "a2140057-eb8c-5b52-a891-cd41551e9f2f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-1$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307848} {:answer/id #uuid "1946a391-5fa3-5df6-98c0-88fabb87a5ac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-1$ and $x=4$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307849}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "22cc68e7-a8d6-5d4a-a344-563e3fab6fdc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-1$ and $x=2$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307845} :answer-field/id #uuid "a2800d50-1de0-5a08-b6c5-2359fb2cd2ed" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307844}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5f2cf18e-79a7-523e-ac97-5665cacdd461" :question/math-academy-id "q-104682" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040492/assets/87c27d8114a9d314fdb93d1722d700a41cdbf82d0af27df58f0188ff67daca05.png)\n\nFor which points does the function $y=f(x)$ above have a jump discontinuity?" :question/worked-solution "The function has a jump discontinuity at $x=-1.$ Checking our continuity conditions, we have\n\n- $f(-1)=2$ exists $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\,(-1)}f(x)$ does not exist $\\,\\,\\times $\n\nSo the second condition failed. It failed because the left and right-sided limits are not equal:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,(-1)^{-}}f(x)=2,\\,\\,{lim}_{x\\to ,(-1)^{+}}f(x)=-1.\n$$\n\nThese limits both exist and are finite, yet they are not equal. Therefore, the discontinuity at $x=-1$ is a jump discontinuity.\n\nIn the same way, the function has a jump discontinuity at $x=2.$ Checking our continuity conditions, we have\n\n- $f(2)=-1$ exists $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to 2}f(x)$ does not exist $\\,\\,\\times $\n\nSo the second condition failed. It failed because the left and right-sided limits are not equal:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}f(x)=1,\\,\\,{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}f(x)=-1.\n$$\n\nThese limits both exist and are finite, yet they are not equal. Therefore, the discontinuity at $x=2$ is a jump discontinuity.\n\nNote that there is also a discontinuity at $x=4,$ but this is not a jump discontinuity."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186307850 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d02297ca-0132-591b-9a69-bc2f93c95e2f" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Jump Discontinuities on a Graph when Multiple Types of Discontinuity Exist"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b866054-3a56-5c16-9126-b3c1b3930f9d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=2$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307852} {:answer/id #uuid "d9e4be1c-f18d-59ec-ab82-2d44a3b0b569" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=0$ and $x=2$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307853} {:answer/id #uuid "31d1a151-3e0a-58e2-94b1-23b0d6410ec8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-2$ and $x=0$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307854} {:answer/id #uuid "99a05726-39e5-5856-a4e4-d26c96192e94" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=0$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307855} {:answer/id #uuid "143a7a97-7cf0-5875-b721-5f482e47caaa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-2$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307856}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1b866054-3a56-5c16-9126-b3c1b3930f9d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=2$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307852} :answer-field/id #uuid "baca7c6c-6162-5f87-b531-990dd4999cf5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307851}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a749e8c5-6c26-5b68-8d7b-2fba028032ee" :question/math-academy-id "q-104689" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040492/assets/2cb9e5fc9fb0e5b1ea95406ba9961cc176a9c637f354b1e8ff2d574cbb1261d6.png)\n\nFor which points does the function $y=f(x)$ above have a jump discontinuity?" :question/worked-solution "The function has a jump discontinuity at $x=2.$ Checking our continuity conditions, we have\n\n- $f(2)=4$ exists $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to 2}f(x)$ does not exist $\\,\\,\\times $\n\nSo the second condition failed. It failed because the left and right-sided limits are not equal:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}f(x)=4,\\,\\,{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}f(x)=1.\n$$\n\nThese limits both exist and are finite, yet they are not equal. Therefore, the discontinuity at $x=2$ is a jump discontinuity.\n\nNote that there is also a discontinuity at $x=0,$ but it is not a jump discontinuity.\n\nHence, the correct answer is $x=2$ only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186307857 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "485fd759-96b3-553f-9eaa-849878c30885" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Jump Discontinuity Given a Function Definition"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "63f578e0-2ff8-593d-ad8e-6f335c8db098" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(x)$ and $g(x)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307859} {:answer/id #uuid "0efa4ec4-a97a-5382-afc6-9bdc8a978f3a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(x)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307860} {:answer/id #uuid "ddc80786-fbd4-51a1-bd40-085ebb2b9029" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$g(x)$ and $h(x)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307861} {:answer/id #uuid "47f0ee7b-0911-5509-b15e-be158a735d4b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$g(x)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307862} {:answer/id #uuid "b1c0d324-463e-5107-9627-2e50ca558f70" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(x)$ and $h(x)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307863}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "63f578e0-2ff8-593d-ad8e-6f335c8db098" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(x)$ and $g(x)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307859} :answer-field/id #uuid "7ce5e39a-870a-599b-9544-4c2d817cf402" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307858}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "b7deaa4c-aeeb-5438-8da3-4314b016404f" :question/math-academy-id "q-104693" :question/problem "Given the functions $f(x),g(x)$ and $h(x)$ below, which of them has a jump discontinuity at $x=4?$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={\\begin{aligned}5, & x<4 \\\\ {x}^{2}-10, & x\\ge 4\\end{aligned} \\\\ g(x) & ={\\begin{aligned}5-2x, & x\\le 4 \\\\ x+3, & x>4\\end{aligned} \\\\ h(x) & ={\\begin{aligned}5x, & x\\ne 4 \\\\ 5, & x=4\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For $y=f(x),$ let's go through our continuity checklist:\n\n- $f(4)={4}^{2}-10=6$ exists $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- Let's now check the left and right limits. We see that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {4}^{-}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to {4}^{-}}5=5\n$$\n\nwhile\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {4}^{+}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to {4}^{+}}({x}^{2}-10)={4}^{2}-10=6.\n$$\n\nSo both the left and right limits exist and are finite, but they are not equal. $\\,\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has a jump discontinuity at $x=4.$\n\nNow, for $y=g(x),$ let's go through our continuity checklist:\n\n- $g(4)=5-2(4)=-3$ exists  $\\,\\,✓$ $\\,\\,\\,$\n\n- Let's now check the left and right limits. We see that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {4}^{-}}g(x)={lim}_{x\\to {4}^{-}}(5-2x)=5-2(4)=-3\n$$\n\nwhile\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {4}^{+}}g(x)={lim}_{x\\to {4}^{+}}(x+3)=4+3=7.\n$$\n\nSo both the left and right limits exist and are finite, but they are not equal. $\\,\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, $g(x)$ has a jump discontinuity at $x=4.$\n\nFinally, for $y=h(x),$ let's go through our continuity checklist:\n\n- $h(4)=5$ exists $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- Let's now check the left and right limits. We see that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {4}^{-}}h(x)={lim}_{x\\to {4}^{-}}5x=5(4)=20\n$$\n\nwhile\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {4}^{+}}h(x)={lim}_{x\\to {4}^{+}}5x=5(4)=20.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the overall limit exists and ${lim}_{x\\to 4}h(x)=20.$\n\n- Finally, we check that the value of the limit equals the value of the function:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 4}h(x)=20\\ne h(4)\\,\\,\\times \n$$\n\nSo the value of the limit does not equal the value of the function.\n\nTherefore, $h(x)$ has a removable discontinuity at $x=4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186307864 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "485fd759-96b3-553f-9eaa-849878c30885" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Jump Discontinuity Given a Function Definition"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0cc8116e-b6ca-5544-8eda-5e18764d86ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(x)$ and $g(x)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307866} {:answer/id #uuid "99e5ff91-b28b-5a6a-801e-0349e4af593d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(x)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307867} {:answer/id #uuid "5f20d27f-238d-5438-9ce5-385f64679e06" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$g(x)$ and $h(x)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307868} {:answer/id #uuid "3e460edf-017e-5f51-84a8-eec176c79128" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$g(x)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307869} {:answer/id #uuid "a5ba140f-edf7-5bf9-9597-79ff9a5ac784" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$h(x)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307870}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "99e5ff91-b28b-5a6a-801e-0349e4af593d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(x)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307867} :answer-field/id #uuid "ab0198b4-3076-5f5c-9fba-8e21dc6f97a3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307865}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "24bfaf28-dbb7-577c-ab56-7ff13a09d906" :question/math-academy-id "q-104701" :question/problem "Given the functions $f(x),g(x)$ and $h(x)$ below, which of them has a jump discontinuity at $x=7?$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={\\begin{aligned}2x-1, & x\\le 7 \\\\ {x}^{2}-30, & x>7,\\end{aligned} \\\\ g(x) & ={\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}, & x<7 \\\\ 5x+14, & x\\ge 7,\\end{aligned} \\\\ h(x) & ={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{x-7}, & x<7 \\\\ \\frac{1}{7}, & x\\ge 7,\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For $f(x),$ let's go through our continuity checklist:\n\n- $f(7)=2(7)-1=13$ exists $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- Let's now check the left and right limits. We see that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,{7}^{-}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to \\,{7}^{-}}(2x-1)=2(7)-1=13\n$$\n\nwhile\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,{7}^{+}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to \\,{7}^{+}}({x}^{2}-30)={7}^{2}-30=19.\n$$\n\nSo both the left and right limits exist and are finite, but they are not equal.$\\,\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has a jump discontinuity at $x=7.$\n\nNow, for $g(x),$ let's go through our continuity checklist:\n\n- $g(7)=5(7)+14=49$ exists $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- Let's now check the left and right limits. We see that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,{7}^{-}}g(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\,{7}^{-}}{x}^{2}=49, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to \\,{7}^{+}}g(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\,{7}^{+}}(5x+14)=5(7)+14=49.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the overall limit exists and ${lim}_{x\\to 7}f(x)=49.\\,\\,✓$ $\\,\\,\\,$\n\n- Finally, we check that the value of the limit equals the value of the function: \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,7}g(x)=49=g(7).\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\nTherefore, $g(x)$ is continuous at $x=7.$\n\nFor $h(x),$ let's go through our continuity checklist:\n\n- $h(7)=\\frac{1}{7}$ exists $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- Let's now check the left and right limits. We see that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,{7}^{-}}h(x)={lim}_{x\\to \\,{7}^{-}}\\frac{1}{x-7}=-\\infty \n$$\n\nwhile\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,{7}^{+}}h(x)={lim}_{x\\to \\,{7}^{+}}\\frac{1}{7}=\\frac{1}{7}.\n$$\n\nSince the left limit is infinite, $h(x)$ does not have a jump discontinuity at $x=7.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186307871 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "07b63c0c-daaa-5f5b-acb3-58d6f36ee869" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Jump Discontinuity on a Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bf22b19f-548b-5eab-98f3-1f873492f7dc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307873} {:answer/id #uuid "55c01282-b83a-5fd5-8adb-3cbcff29b2cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307874} {:answer/id #uuid "63277236-585a-580d-bee0-d62bddfcbe9e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307875} {:answer/id #uuid "d815f591-412a-5394-a1bf-040363ef8624" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307876} {:answer/id #uuid "64798606-8a72-5282-b35b-be61e6948047" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307877}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "55c01282-b83a-5fd5-8adb-3cbcff29b2cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307874} :answer-field/id #uuid "008aafcf-5d72-5056-b337-44be156eac9c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307872}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1aadd872-6e8c-538b-9d15-8a8234678b76" :question/math-academy-id "q-38712" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040492/assets/4a9a1be6abdaf5104cecf7544dc77687db2892de1e3b39faab03f99b84003c03.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true regarding the function $y=f(x)$ plotted above?\n\n- It has a jump discontinuity at $x=2.$\n- It has a point discontinuity at $x=2.$\n- It is continuous at $x=2.$" :question/worked-solution "The function has a jump discontinuity at $x=2.$ Checking our conditions, we see that\n\n- $f(2)=2$ exists, $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to 2}f(x)$ does not exist $\\,\\,\\times $\n\nSo the second condition failed. It failed because the left and right-sided limits are not equal:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}f(x)=2,\\,\\,{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}f(x)=3.\n$$\n\nThese limits both exist and are finite, yet they are not equal. Therefore, the discontinuity at $x=2$ is a jump discontinuity.\n\nConsequently, I is true, and II and III are false."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186307878 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "485fd759-96b3-553f-9eaa-849878c30885" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Jump Discontinuity Given a Function Definition"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b44cb702-8f3c-5b04-853e-bfabee62c659" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(x)$, $g(x)$ and $h(x)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307880} {:answer/id #uuid "7e3968dc-b090-5f57-ab40-db2960ec7307" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(x)$ and $g(x)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307881} {:answer/id #uuid "bba64cc2-80b9-59d0-b4a6-7d13e912354c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(x)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307882} {:answer/id #uuid "25929590-3856-50c1-b9e9-a3186bd1bc7e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$g(x)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307883} {:answer/id #uuid "0ef59895-f90b-54c2-8f23-3eecda3fd45b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$h(x)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307884}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bba64cc2-80b9-59d0-b4a6-7d13e912354c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(x)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307882} :answer-field/id #uuid "103ca529-129c-538e-bf33-e84acd3fac78" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307879}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c3ff5938-5386-52c8-baaa-7140885bcc81" :question/math-academy-id "q-48112" :question/problem "Given the functions $f(x),g(x)$ and $h(x)$ below, which of them has a jump discontinuity at $x=1?$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={\\begin{aligned}x+4, & x\\le 1 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}+1, & x>1,\\end{aligned} \\\\ g(x) & ={\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+1, & x<1 \\\\ \\sqrt{x+3}, & x\\ge 1,\\end{aligned} \\\\ h(x) & ={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{x+3}, & x\\ne 1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}, & x=1,\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For $f(x),$ let's go through our continuity checklist:\n\n- $f(1)=1+4=5$ exists $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- Let's now check the left and right limits. We see that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{-}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{-}}(x+4)=1+4=5\n$$\n\nwhile\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{+}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{+}}(3{x}^{2}+1)=3(1)^{2}+1=4.\n$$\n\nSo both the left and right limits exist and are finite, but they are not equal.$\\,\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has a jump discontinuity at $x=1.$\n\nNow, for $g(x),$ let's go through our continuity checklist:\n\n- $g(1)=\\sqrt{1+3}=2$ exists $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- Let's now check the left and right limits. We see that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{-}}g(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{-}}({x}^{2}+1)=(1)^{2}+1=2, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{+}}g(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{+}}(\\sqrt{x+3})=\\sqrt{1+3}=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the overall limit exists and ${lim}_{x\\to 1}f(x)=2.\\,\\,✓$ $\\,\\,\\,$\n\n- Finally, we check that the value of the limit equals the value of the function: \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,1}g(x)=2=g(1).\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\nTherefore, $g(x)$ is continuous at $x=1.$\n\nFor $h(x),$ let's go through our continuity checklist:\n\n- $h(1)=\\frac{1}{2}$ exists $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- Let's now check the left and right limits. We see that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{-}}h(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{-}}\\frac{1}{x+3}=\\frac{1}{4}, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{+}}h(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{+}}\\frac{1}{x+3}=\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the overall limit exists and ${lim}_{x\\to 1}f(x)=\\frac{1}{4}.\\,\\,✓$ $\\,\\,\\,$\n\n- Finally, we check that the value of the limit equals the value of the function: \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,1}h(x)=\\frac{1}{4}\\ne h(1).\\,\\,\\times \n$$\n\nSo the value of the limit does not equal the value of the function.\n\nTherefore, $h(x)$ has a removable discontinuity at $x=1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186307885 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "485fd759-96b3-553f-9eaa-849878c30885" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Jump Discontinuity Given a Function Definition"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5a91ab59-2d52-5e73-878c-9d2392328831" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(x)$, $g(x)$ and $h(x)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307887} {:answer/id #uuid "53a6b913-bad1-5f2d-a0eb-37929031c552" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(x)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307888} {:answer/id #uuid "8dc4fe56-5644-5bdb-84f0-20aee22b633a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$g(x)$ and $h(x)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307889} {:answer/id #uuid "61eeecfc-8eb5-5ede-9d48-b1980d456122" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$g(x)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307890} {:answer/id #uuid "c448d673-5039-5f64-bb1b-14c2cf8f90df" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$h(x)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307891}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c448d673-5039-5f64-bb1b-14c2cf8f90df" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$h(x)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307891} :answer-field/id #uuid "e09ca5b9-6e5f-57a6-bc4a-e53fd0094a8a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307886}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c341ed7c-c896-5097-a1a6-74e007b32c66" :question/math-academy-id "q-48114" :question/problem "Given the functions $f(x),g(x)$ and $h(x)$ below, which of them has a jump discontinuity at $x=0?$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-3x, & x\\ne 0 \\\\ 0, & x=0,\\end{aligned} \\\\ g(x) & ={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+1}, & x\\ne 0 \\\\ 2, & x=0,\\end{aligned} \\\\ h(x) & ={\\begin{aligned}3, & x<0 \\\\ \\sqrt{x+4}, & x\\ge 0.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For $y=f(x),$ let's go through our continuity checklist:\n\n- $f(0)=0$ exists $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- Let's now check the left and right limits. We see that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{-}}({x}^{2}-3x)={0}^{2}-3(0)=0, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{+}}({x}^{2}-3x)={0}^{2}-3(0)=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the overall limit exists and ${lim}_{x\\to \\,0}f(x)=0.\\,\\,✓$\n\n- Finally, we check that the value of the limit equals the value of the function:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,0}f(x)=0=f(0).\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=0.$\n\nNow, for $y=g(x),$ let's go through our continuity checklist:\n\n- $g(0)=2$ exists $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- Let's now check the left and right limits. We see that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{-}}g(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{-}}(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+1})=\\frac{1}{{0}^{2}+1}=1, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{+}}g(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{+}}(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+1})=\\frac{1}{{0}^{2}+1}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the overall limit exists and ${lim}_{x\\to \\,0}g(x)=1.$\n\n- Finally, we check that the value of the limit equals the value of the function: \n\n$$\ng(0)=2\\ne {lim}_{x\\to \\,0}g(x).\\,\\,\\times \n$$\n\nSo the value of the limit does not equal the value of the function.\n\nTherefore, $g(x)$ has a removable discontinuity at $x=0.$\n\nFinally, for $y=h(x),$ let's go through our continuity checklist:\n\n- $h(0)=\\sqrt{0+4}=2$ exists $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- Let's now check the left and right limits. We see that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{-}}h(x)={lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{-}}(3)=3\n$$\n\nwhile\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{+}}h(x)={lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{+}}(\\sqrt{x+4})=\\sqrt{0+4}=2.\n$$\n\nSo both the left and right limits exist and are finite, but they are not equal.$\\,\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, $h(x)$ has a jump discontinuity at $x=0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129736 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d02297ca-0132-591b-9a69-bc2f93c95e2f" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Jump Discontinuities on a Graph when Multiple Types of Discontinuity Exist"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16021de0-3759-5cec-899e-25d4ab8e80c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-3$ and $x=1$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307893} {:answer/id #uuid "e02036d6-7618-546a-a0a5-40e8b80c660b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=1$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307894} {:answer/id #uuid "0132e7a2-61b3-5fb9-88a1-a09301bb1c2d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=0$ and $x=1$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307895} {:answer/id #uuid "32ca1c72-6a60-59c6-9b87-1fdefa4b6c21" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-3$, $x=0$ and $x=1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307896} {:answer/id #uuid "7b9c83c8-6024-50c0-ac7d-554313fab134" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-3$  only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307897}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "16021de0-3759-5cec-899e-25d4ab8e80c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-3$ and $x=1$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307893} :answer-field/id #uuid "47d25245-8d65-5234-a1a9-df1bb8893443" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307892}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2ed69968-e37f-592e-8251-96c027066333" :question/math-academy-id "q-48119" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040492/assets/f1e1a140e78362efddf48ba6744e2f88d109000fba8eae97818b024c2d249ad5.png)\n\nFor which points does the function $y=f(x)$ above have a jump discontinuity?" :question/worked-solution "The function has a jump discontinuity at $x=-3.$ Checking our continuity conditions, we have\n\n- $f(-3)=1$ exists $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to -3}f(x)$ does not exist $\\,\\,\\times $\n\nSo the second condition failed. It failed because the left and right-sided limits are not equal:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -{3}^{-}}f(x)=-1,\\,\\,{lim}_{x\\to -{3}^{+}}f(x)=1.\n$$\n\nThese limits both exist and are finite, yet they are not equal. Therefore, the discontinuity at $x=-3$ is a jump discontinuity.\n\nIn the same way, the function has a jump discontinuity at $x=1.$ Checking our continuity conditions, we have\n\n- $f(1)=0$ exists $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to 1}f(x)$ does not exist $\\,\\,\\times $\n\nSo the second condition failed. It failed because the left and right-sided limits are not equal:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x)=-1,\\,\\,{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x)=0.\n$$\n\nThese limits both exist and are finite, yet they are not equal. Therefore, the discontinuity at $x=1$ is a jump discontinuity.\n\nNote that there is also a discontinuity at $x=0,$ but this is not a jump discontinuity."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186307898 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d02297ca-0132-591b-9a69-bc2f93c95e2f" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Jump Discontinuities on a Graph when Multiple Types of Discontinuity Exist"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4ec29fc9-d967-5bd7-9a7e-99cf6038e527" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-5$ and $x=1$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307900} {:answer/id #uuid "0831cc40-029b-5e26-a001-abf6526242b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-5$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307901} {:answer/id #uuid "76d1bac2-4489-511e-bba3-eb3fc131a53e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-5$ and $x=-1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307902} {:answer/id #uuid "2a222f44-9bf3-57a5-a627-f3cdc3c21778" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=1$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307903} {:answer/id #uuid "22e230bb-d2ef-5341-b366-f2db4ef4e1ce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-1$ and $x=1$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307904}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2a222f44-9bf3-57a5-a627-f3cdc3c21778" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=1$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307903} :answer-field/id #uuid "9a697b6b-3770-50fb-a4fe-206ffb6140f4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307899}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d48ac5a6-0498-5bfc-aced-fcaca9891cf6" :question/math-academy-id "q-48124" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040492/assets/83c19d6864e09383d64946b32d2ef9b98fc773c030f9922ad8843e54c60399d6.png)\n\nFor which points does the function $y=f(x)$ above have a jump discontinuity?" :question/worked-solution "The function has a jump discontinuity at $x=1.$ Checking our continuity conditions, we have\n\n- $f(1)=1$ exists $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to f(x)}$ does not exist $\\,\\,\\times $\n\nSo the second condition failed. It failed because the left and right-sided limits are not equal:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x)=2,\\,\\,{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x)=1.\n$$\n\nThese limits both exist and are finite, yet they are not equal. Therefore, the discontinuity at $x=1$ is a jump discontinuity.\n\nNote that there are also discontinuities at $x=-5$ and $x=-1,$ but they are not jump discontinuities.\n\nHence, the correct answer is $x=1$ only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129739 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "07b63c0c-daaa-5f5b-acb3-58d6f36ee869" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Jump Discontinuity on a Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cb6cf62e-f836-5d83-93d7-91f033ea47af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2003/Source/Images/q-48141-a-5.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128229} {:answer/id #uuid "f744a4a4-3f83-5c2f-a569-c5509fec0768" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2003/Source/Images/q-48141-a-1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128230} {:answer/id #uuid "29f5b25b-8f02-550a-91ac-2fcfadfd9a18" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2003/Source/Images/q-48141-a-2.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128231} {:answer/id #uuid "2e8d6fd5-fdab-5409-979f-59fe03dad2f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2003/Source/Images/q-48141-a-4.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128232} {:answer/id #uuid "657e4a9c-fb02-5d63-a186-2be2b2adf030" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2003/Source/Images/q-48141-a-3.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128233}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f744a4a4-3f83-5c2f-a569-c5509fec0768" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2003/Source/Images/q-48141-a-1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128230} :answer-field/id #uuid "3484838d-1381-55df-849e-8d643c3bfe62" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128228}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d78b5a6f-6787-5993-a28a-a27307be4e0b" :question/math-academy-id "q-48141" :question/problem "Which of the following functions has a jump discontinuity at $x=-1?$" :question/worked-solution "The function $y=f(x)$ has a jump discontinuity at $x=-1.$ Checking our conditions, we see that\n\n- $f(-1)=2$ exists $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to -1}f(x)$ does not exist $\\,\\,\\times $\n\nSo the second condition failed. It failed because the left and right-sided limits are not equal:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -{1}^{-}}f(x)=2,\\,\\,{lim}_{x\\to -{1}^{+}}f(x)=4.\n$$\n\nThese limits both exist and are finite, yet they are not equal. Therefore, the discontinuity at $x=-1$ is a jump discontinuity.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040492/assets/8961fba5e89b2c6e85879f448806b0cc348cf9d271e18606ad318c1b8bf9e13c.png)\n\nNotice that the functions $h(x)$ and $p(x)$ are continuous at $x=-1,$ while $g(x)$ and $q(x)$ both have a discontinuity at $x=-1,$ but they are *not* jump discontinuities."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186307905 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "485fd759-96b3-553f-9eaa-849878c30885" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Jump Discontinuity Given a Function Definition"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9f4e3389-fc6b-50ca-8bf3-6a31009db2ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307907} {:answer/id #uuid "db7df812-7d28-53f8-bfb4-16c2272ff7e7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307908} {:answer/id #uuid "da430fae-ce08-5b61-9ef6-bbad81747348" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307909} {:answer/id #uuid "de1cc7da-6d7a-5770-9d82-5159bebc56a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307910} {:answer/id #uuid "b5783c6d-07ba-5cac-ba4a-f280add334a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307911}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "db7df812-7d28-53f8-bfb4-16c2272ff7e7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307908} :answer-field/id #uuid "103287d0-37e3-5298-97d8-6af14a575728" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307906}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "449cb233-0a42-5258-ab1c-39119094e289" :question/math-academy-id "q-48167" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $f(x)$ below?\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+3, & x<1 \\\\ 1-{x}^{3}, & x\\ge 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- It has a jump discontinuity at $x=1.$\n- It has a removable discontinuity at $x=1.$\n- It's continuous at $x=1.$" :question/worked-solution "For $x=1,$ let's go through our continuity checklist:\n\n- $f(1)=1-{1}^{3}=0$ exists $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- Let's now check the left and right limits. We see that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}({x}^{2}+3)=({1}^{2}+3)=4\n$$\n\nwhile \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}(1-{x}^{3})=(1-{1}^{3})=0.\n$$\n\nSo both the left and right limits exist and are finite, but they are not equal. $\\,\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has a jump discontinuity at $x=1.$\n\nHence, I is true, while II and III are false."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186307912 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "07b63c0c-daaa-5f5b-acb3-58d6f36ee869" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Jump Discontinuity on a Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d33fa08c-b5fe-556c-a129-d1c033ee5349" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307914} {:answer/id #uuid "6a0a698b-3217-5b50-9a60-41731e74c9d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307915} {:answer/id #uuid "9b8fab6f-aca5-52b4-b15d-92ee62b3dce4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307916} {:answer/id #uuid "affa023e-bb08-5ae6-8a22-9a891c5ca989" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307917} {:answer/id #uuid "51e4dd83-11ee-5e48-bd18-8e4ca3aa0536" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307918}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6a0a698b-3217-5b50-9a60-41731e74c9d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307915} :answer-field/id #uuid "48a2be45-bbd2-5c69-a58d-3dbde385d018" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307913}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "825ab862-b738-5389-82d3-382684f52334" :question/math-academy-id "q-48185" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040492/assets/786d223be90c13335ab80672631dcbef25297688ae9e318928e744ebbf8a5cdf.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true regarding the function $y=f(x)$ plotted above?\n\n- It has a jump discontinuity at $x=-1.$\n- It has a removable discontinuity at $x=-1.$\n- It is continuous at $x=-1.$" :question/worked-solution "The function has a jump discontinuity at $x=-1.$ Checking our conditions, we see that\n\n- $f(-1)=2$ exists, $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to -1}f(x)$ does not exist $\\,\\,\\times $\n\nSo the second condition failed. It failed because the left and right-sided limits are not equal:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{-}}f(x)=1,\\,\\,{lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{+}}f(x)=2.\n$$\n\nThese limits both exist and are finite, yet they are not equal. Therefore, the discontinuity at $x=-1$ is a jump discontinuity.\n\nConsequently, I is true, and II and III are false."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186307919 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "07b63c0c-daaa-5f5b-acb3-58d6f36ee869" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Jump Discontinuity on a Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "eb331c7b-7a6d-53a1-9717-62f4a371ae7c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307921} {:answer/id #uuid "6294b263-59a7-56d7-84d5-4f97b2ef5fcb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307922} {:answer/id #uuid "485cae68-ccb0-5289-aec8-cee3e919c42f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307923} {:answer/id #uuid "f6546cb1-dd43-5bfc-adb4-77f5b86a9bc6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307924} {:answer/id #uuid "d00ccab5-32c8-5994-ae8f-21a4e25027c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307925}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d00ccab5-32c8-5994-ae8f-21a4e25027c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307925} :answer-field/id #uuid "940e0936-d7e3-5b08-9611-25bcabaceac3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307920}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ed867e40-29b3-530b-96c3-6f5fbbb12043" :question/math-academy-id "q-48187" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040492/assets/034e2813b404338a1b8d0ecfdb509781f7917e356155474d788d8bd85df0f221.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true regarding the function $y=f(x)$ plotted above?\n\n- It has a point discontinuity at $x=-2.$\n- It has a jump discontinuity at $x=-2.$\n- It is continuous at $x=-2.$" :question/worked-solution "The function has a jump discontinuity at $x=-2.$ Checking our conditions, we see that\n\n- $f(-2)=2$ exists, $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to -2}f(x)$ does not exist $\\,\\,\\times $\n\nSo the second condition failed. It failed because the left and right-sided limits are not equal:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (-2)^{-}}f(x)=1,\\,\\,{lim}_{x\\to (-2)^{+}}f(x)=2.\n$$\n\nThese limits both exist and are finite, yet they are not equal. Therefore, the discontinuity at $x=-2$ is a jump discontinuity.\n\nConsequently, II is true, and I and III are false."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186307926 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d02297ca-0132-591b-9a69-bc2f93c95e2f" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Jump Discontinuities on a Graph when Multiple Types of Discontinuity Exist"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f4aa3bbf-6629-5691-8691-aa382586d5b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-3$ and $x=-1$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307928} {:answer/id #uuid "2971b923-8552-52dd-8f17-04cae38434ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-3$ and $x=3$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307929} {:answer/id #uuid "44e4cfb3-181e-57c6-9513-f8effb4326b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-3$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307930} {:answer/id #uuid "a878fbd6-6d99-5364-9309-30c7f90fa3b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=3$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307931} {:answer/id #uuid "180db685-934f-5bb4-9eea-1b34d27edfa3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-1$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307932}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "180db685-934f-5bb4-9eea-1b34d27edfa3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-1$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307932} :answer-field/id #uuid "b0bbe0b0-9643-5b69-8842-2dc03d3ff3a1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307927}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f9a882dc-93e1-528d-86d1-3ebbbceca8bd" :question/math-academy-id "q-73046" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040492/assets/adec7c5c587dcaf899d53432f724a52c0b59d40dfc9d5fafc6cfca92ca3e052e.png)\n\nFor which points does the function $y=f(x)$ above have a jump discontinuity?" :question/worked-solution "The function has a jump discontinuity at $x=-1.$ Checking our conditions, we have\n\n- $f(-1)=4$ exists $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to -1}f(x)$ does not exist $\\,\\,\\times $\n\nSo the second condition failed. It failed because the left and right-sided limits are not equal:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{-}}f(x)=4,\\,\\,{lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{+}}f(x)=2.\n$$\n\nThese limits both exist and are finite, yet they are not equal. Therefore, the discontinuity at $x=-1$ is a jump discontinuity.\n\nNote that there are also discontinuities at $x=-3$ and $x=3,$ but they are not jump discontinuities.\n\nHence, the correct answer is $x=-1$ only."}]}
